135
00:09:40.233 --> 00:09:42.020
(narrator) The bridge, private isn’t enough.

136
00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:45.051
(narrator) Your hero thinks every field is private, so it’s hidden.

137
00:09:45.051 --> 00:09:52.035
(narrator) A classified code file shows the private fields, but the public checkout method returns a PayPalCharge and takes a PayPalAccount.

138
00:09:52.035 --> 00:09:57.881
(narrator) Only these public types are outlined in red, and paper slips with those names slide out of the file: leaked.

139
00:09:57.881 --> 00:10:03.132
(narrator) Private fields get a stamp, not enough; hiding decisions lights up gold beside a sealed envelope.

140
00:10:03.132 --> 00:10:08.546
(narrator) Then a client application sends a request to a server, with a separate response arrow pointing back.

141
00:10:08.546 --> 00:10:13.256
(narrator) Their visible contract includes the protocol: stateless or stateful, callers must know.

142
00:10:13.256 --> 00:10:17.965
(narrator) The database choice, SQL or NoSQL, stays inside the server boundary as a hidden detail.

143
00:10:18.547 --> 00:10:22.287
♪ “I made every field private. So it’s hidden, right?” ♪

144
00:10:22.737 --> 00:10:25.587
♪ But checkout still returns a PayPal Charge: ♪

145
00:10:25.587 --> 00:10:28.887
